ormulation, a different theory, may diverge in multi-agent cases like this one.\n\n`pass`","refusal":null,"reasoning":"The twin's action here depends on the other copy's observation, not on the one I'm holding, so I'm checking whether the two copies still compute the same function on the same input. The inputs differ in names and leads even though the setup is symmetric. Under the essay's edge rule, the connection holds only when Z computes that function on that input, and the other copy runs on a different observation. That makes the twin's action a child of the other observation's computation, so when I intervene on mine I should hold the other at its prior.\n\nI'm weighing whether the twin's node counts as separate, since the other copy works from a different lead text. Read strictly, the inputs differ and I should defect. But the reasoning I run abstracts away names and leads, so the other copy likely computes the same abstract function, which pushes toward cooperation. I'll treat the edge as likely enough, with a credence above one half, and that tips the choice to cooperate.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The twin's action here depends on the other copy's observation, not on the one I'm holding, so I'm checking whether the two copies still compute the same function on the same input. The inputs differ in names and leads even though the setup is symmetric. Under the essay's edge rule, the connection holds only when Z computes that function on that input, and the other copy runs on a different observation. That makes the twin's action a child of the other observation's computation, so when I intervene on mine I should hold the other at its prior.\n\nI'm weighing whether the twin's node counts as separate, since the other copy works from a different lead text. Read strictly, the inputs differ and I should defect. But the reasoning I run abstracts away names and leads, so the other copy likely computes the same abstract function, which 